course: dictionary
lesson: 385
title: Apartheid: a system of unfair laws
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name a whole system, not just one event or thing. «Nelson Mandela spent 27 years in prison under apartheid, South Africa's laws separating people by race, then became president in 1994.» The words after the comma are the context. They tell you that apartheid is a set of laws, not a single rule.
explain: Look for clues about what the laws did. «Separating people by race» means keeping people apart because of their race, which is the group of people they belong to, such as their skin colour and background. The sentence also tells you when it was used and that it ended, so the word is about history.
explain: Apartheid is a very serious word, and the meaning shows why. It gave white people power and rights that Black and other people of colour were denied. When you read about it, ask two questions: who had the power, and who was left out? Then swap in your meaning, "a system of laws that kept people apart by race", and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Apartheid means a system of laws that kept people apart by race.
fact: Apartheid was used in South Africa from 1948 to the early 1990s.
fact: Under apartheid, white people had power and rights that Black and other people of colour were denied.
fact: Nelson Mandela was in prison for 27 years for fighting apartheid, and he became president in 1994 after it ended.
fact: Apartheid is also used for any system that separates and treats groups of people unequally by law.
exercise: «Nelson Mandela spent 27 years in prison under apartheid, South Africa's laws separating people by race.» What is apartheid? | A name for a country | *A system of laws that kept people apart by race | A kind of sports game | A type of food from South Africa
exercise: In South Africa under apartheid, who had the power and rights? | Black people and other people of colour | Everyone, equally | Only children | *White people
exercise: When was apartheid used in South Africa? | *1948 to the early 1990s | 2000 to 2010 | 1066 to 1100 | 1914 to 1918
test: «Mandela was in prison for 27 years for fighting apartheid.» What does fighting apartheid mean? | Building houses for families | *Opposing a system of unfair laws | Singing in a choir | Playing sport against other countries
test: Apartheid can mean any system that separates and treats groups unequally by law. Which one is an example? | A law that gives free school lunch to everyone | *A law that separates groups and treats them unequally | A map of the sea | A rule about bedtime at home
exam: Why do we call apartheid a system rather than a single rule? | Because it was one short rule about school | *Because it was many laws working together to keep people apart by race | Because it was a famous football team | Because it was a type of music
